The organizer sends a long plain-text catalog of books donated
for a sale. You want to check its heading, see whether there is a
closing note, and estimate how much you have left to read. You
do not want the whole catalog filling the terminal, nor do you
want to open it in an editor just to look. A few small commands
let you choose what to inspect. They also force you to say what
you mean by a ``line'' or a ``character,'' distinctions that
matter as soon as a file contains more than unaccented English.

\begin{objectives}
\item View the beginning, end, or a bounded section of a text file.
\item Browse with a pager if one is available, without changing the file.
\item Choose between newline, word, byte, and character counts.
\end{objectives}

\section{Prepare a catalog that fits on a page}
\label{sec:18-setup}
The small catalog here makes it possible to check every answer.
Use \cmd|sh|, and start in a new private directory. The first
line stores and prints the directory path; stop if it doesn't
print a new path. Record that path and run the second command
only after the setup succeeds. \cmd|>| opens the named output,
truncating an existing file, so never use a filename you need to keep.

\begin{terminal}
lab=$(mktemp -d) && cd "$lab" && pwd
printf '%s\n' 'Library sale' 'Saturday' 'Art books' \
  'Field guides' 'Mystery novels' 'End of list' > catalog.txt
\end{terminal}

The backslash immediately before the newline continues the
command. Nothing should come after it on that physical line.
The format \cmd|'%s\n'| writes one newline after each phrase,
and each quoted phrase becomes one data argument. Notice that
the file has six lines but only three lines list types of
books. A line count will not automatically decide which
lines belong to the catalog body.

\section{Open the front or the back}
\label{sec:18-ends}
\IndexCommand{head}
\IndexCommand{tail}
\begin{terminal}
head -n 2 catalog.txt
tail -n 2 catalog.txt
\end{terminal}

The number argument to \cmd|-n| selects how many lines to
print. For \cmd|head|, expect \texttt{Library sale} and
\texttt{Saturday}; for \cmd|tail|, expect \texttt{Mystery novels}
and \texttt{End of list}. There is no guarantee that a file
has at least the requested number of lines. A short file can
produce fewer without anything being wrong. Use the explicit
\cmd|-n| forms instead of depending on older utility syntax,
which varies. For a report you intend to share, verify the
sample is the part you need, not merely a plausible-looking
chunk at the right end.

\begin{terminal}
tail -n +3 catalog.txt | head -n 3
\end{terminal}

The plus before the first count changes it into a start
position, with the first line numbered one. \cmd|tail|
sends lines three through six into the pipe. \cmd|head|
returns at most the first three lines of that stream. Expect
\texttt{Art books}, \texttt{Field guides}, and
\texttt{Mystery novels}. Figure~\ref{fig:18-window} shows
the window. An early-finishing reader can cause a producer's
write to fail when there's more output, so on a large file
an early cutoff isn't a test of whether the entire input
was read successfully. See Chapter~\ref{ch:17} for that
pipeline distinction.

\begin{figure}[htbp]
\centering\input{figures/chapter-18/window.tikz}
\caption{Start at line three, then take no more than three lines.}
\label{fig:18-window}
\VisualDescription{Lines one and two are outside the selection because tail skips them. Lines three, four, and five pass through head. Head leaves out the closing sixth line.}
\end{figure}

\begin{quicktable}{M{1.65in}Y}
\toprule
Command form & Question it answers \\
\midrule
head -n 2 file & What do the first two lines say, or what's present if there are fewer? \\
tail -n 2 file & What do the last two lines say, or what's present if there are fewer? \\
tail -n +3 file & What is present starting at the third line? \\
less file & What can I see by paging through the named text without altering it? \\
\bottomrule
\end{quicktable}

\section{Read at your own speed}
\label{sec:18-pager}
\IndexTerm{pager}
\IndexCommand{less}
If \cmd|less| is installed, it is a \emph{pager}: it displays
some text at a time and waits for navigation keys. It isn't a
text editor. Check availability without installing anything:

\begin{terminal}
command -v less
\end{terminal}

\cmd|command -v| asks the shell how it would find a command
of that name. If the lookup fails, skip the optional pager
example; \cmd|head| and \cmd|tail| still work. \cmd|more|
is the POSIX pager; check \cmd|man more| for your local keys
and behavior. If \cmd|less| is available, run:

\begin{terminal}
less catalog.txt
\end{terminal}

Use Space to move forward by a screen and \texttt{b} to move
backward by a screen. Press \texttt{q} to quit. The short
catalog may already be visible in its entirety; the exercise
trains you to recognize that the pager, not the shell, has
control. Some configurations exit immediately for a short
file. If you do see the shell prompt already, don't type
\texttt{q} there. To search within an open \cmd|less| session,
press \texttt{/}, type \texttt{guides}, and press \EnterKey.
Here \texttt{guides} has no special pattern characters.
Navigation and searching do not edit the catalog. They also
do not produce a new list of matches to feed into another
tool: Chapter~\ref{ch:19} covers that different task.

\begin{figure}[htbp]
\centering\input{figures/chapter-18/pager.tikz}
\caption{A pager moves the view over the text, not the text itself.}
\label{fig:18-pager}
\VisualDescription{A file feeds a pager and the pager supplies a screenful. Navigation keys move from the reader back into the pager, with no write connection from the pager to the file.}
\end{figure}

When the input file isn't known to be text, start with the
file identification approach in Chapter~\ref{ch:7}. Don't
dump an unknown program or media file onto the terminal with
\cmd|cat| just to see what it is. A pager is most suitable
for text you intend to read. Even for text, a file may contain
tabs, unusually long lines, or control characters. A very
long line may wrap across several screen rows even though
it counts as one newline-terminated line. The number of
screen rows isn't a reliable way to count records.

\section{Choose the unit before counting}
\label{sec:18-counts}
\IndexTerm{newline}
\IndexTerm{byte}
\IndexCommand{wc}
\begin{terminal}
wc -l -w -c catalog.txt
wc -l < catalog.txt
\end{terminal}

The first report shows, in this order, six newlines, twelve
words, seventy-two bytes, and the operand name. Spacing can
vary. A word in the utility's sense is a nonempty sequence
delimited by whitespace, not a judgment about grammar.
The narrow count in the second line shows six with no
filename because \cmd|<| connects standard input to the
file and no operand names it. In that command \cmd|-l|
counts newlines, not displayed rows or phrases. This is why
you should say which unit you intend, rather than calling
\cmd|wc| without options and guessing what its columns mean.

\begin{terminal}
printf '%s' 'last but not terminated' > partial.txt
wc -l partial.txt
wc -w partial.txt
\end{terminal}

There is no \cmd|\n| in this format, so the file has text
but no final newline. On the tested implementation the
newline count is zero and the word count is four. Such a
file isn't a POSIX text file, whose lines end in newline
characters, even though an editor may display the fragment
as a line. If someone sends a report and your count is off
by one, inspect the line endings and how the file was made
before deciding that a record was lost. Don't rewrite the
file merely to reconcile two counts taken in different units.

\begin{quicktable}{M{1in}Y}
\toprule
Option & Unit and common mistake \\
\midrule
-l & Newlines, not the number of visually wrapped rows or unterminated fragments. \\
-w & Whitespace-separated words under the utility's rules, not grammatical words. \\
-c & Bytes, including the bytes for spaces and line endings, not displayed characters. \\
-m & Characters in the active locale, not bytes or visual columns. \\
\bottomrule
\end{quicktable}

\section{When bytes and characters diverge}
\label{sec:18-unicode}
\IndexTerm{Unicode}
\IndexTerm{UTF-8}
\IndexTerm{locale}
Most modern desktop systems can read UTF-8, an encoding
of Unicode characters as byte sequences. In UTF-8 an
unaccented Latin letter used in the fixture takes one
byte; \texttt{é} as a single precomposed character takes
two. To make the test independent of how your PDF viewer
copies accented text, type the following ASCII command.
The \cmd|\303\251| escapes in the \cmd|printf| format name
the two bytes by their octal values (base eight). \cmd|\n|
adds one more byte for a newline.

\begin{terminal}
printf 'Caf\303\251\n' > accent.txt
wc -c < accent.txt
locale charmap
wc -m < accent.txt
\end{terminal}

The byte count is six. \cmd|locale charmap| asks which
character encoding the current locale uses. If it reports
UTF-8, a conforming character count for this well-formed
fixture is five: four letters and a newline. If it reports
another encoding, don't expect five. To investigate, use
\cmd|locale -a| to list installed locales and look for a
UTF-8 one. For example, only if that exact name is available,
\cmd|LC_ALL=C.UTF-8 wc -m < accent.txt| selects it just for
that invocation. Locale names differ: do not assume
\texttt{C.UTF-8} exists on macOS or any BSD system. You
don't need to change your login settings or install anything
to verify the byte count.

\begin{figure}[htbp]
\centering\input{figures/chapter-18/units.tikz}
\caption{Two UTF-8 bytes can represent one character.}
\label{fig:18-units}
\VisualDescription{The byte sequence for C, a, f, accented e, and newline has six bytes. The accented e occupies two adjacent bytes while each other character occupies one.}
\end{figure}

Even characters and things a reader perceives as letters
can diverge. An accented letter can also be encoded as an
unaccented letter followed by a separate combining accent.
This can look the same but count differently. Other
characters may take two screen columns; some marks take
no additional column. Use bytes when budgeting a transfer,
newlines when counting newline-delimited records, and
characters only when you know which encoding is being
read. If a form specifies a limit without saying whether
it counts bytes, characters, or something else, check its
rules rather than forcing your preferred count to agree.

\begin{dialoguebox}{How long is the line?}
\Brian{A file with four letters has six bytes. Is the counter including a secret letter?}
\Stephen{It includes the newline, and one of the letters uses two UTF-8 bytes.}
\Brian{Then should I count characters and subtract the newline to find the number of letters?}
\Stephen{For this example that works, but it isn't a reliable rule for all text.}
\Brian{What if I type the unaccented letter and then add the accent? It looks the same.}
\Stephen{It can be stored as two characters instead of one. The screen isn't showing the byte boundaries.}
\Brian{So a word limit, a character limit, and a storage limit can disagree honestly.}
\Stephen{Yes. Ask which one the task actually needs.}
\DialogueConclusion{Choose the unit before trusting the count.}
\end{dialoguebox}

\section{Read a difficult count from both directions}
\label{sec:18-review}
Imagine someone says the last line of a file is missing.
Start by asking which observation prompted that conclusion.
If they browsed on a narrow screen, a single long line
may have wrapped over several rows. If they counted
newlines, an unterminated final fragment may explain the
difference. If they expected a closing marker, \cmd|tail|
can tell you whether the ending actually contains it.
The file can fail one of these expectations without
anything having been deleted. Use a second observation to
test the specific assumption.

For instance, view the beginning of the catalog and then
count only the bytes in that sample:

\begin{terminal}
head -n 1 catalog.txt | wc -c
head -n 1 catalog.txt | wc -w
\end{terminal}

Expect thirteen bytes from \texttt{Library sale} with
its space and newline, and two words. The two readings
describe the same line. If you include the filename as a
\cmd|wc| operand on the right by mistake, you'll get a
count for the entire file, which is not the requested
sample. Keep the command that chooses the sample separate
from the command that measures it. If the count differs,
inspect the sample by running the producer alone before
blaming the count's unit or locale.

Look at the last line of an unfamiliar export before
passing it to a filtering tool. It may be a total,
timestamp, or footer rather than another row of data.
Similarly, \cmd|head -n 1| can tell you whether there's
a header, but not whether all following rows use that
same format. The first and last screens are useful samples,
not substitutes for validating a whole file. This is
especially important with a format like CSV where a
logical record can contain embedded newlines and therefore
can occupy more than one physical line. Chapter~\ref{ch:24}
deals with that format separately.

For a final comparison, make a second accent example with
an unaccented \texttt{e} followed by a combining acute
accent. The accent is encoded as octal bytes
\cmd|\314\201|. As before, the file is a new lab fixture
and the byte escapes let you type only ASCII at the prompt.

\begin{terminal}
printf 'Cafe\314\201\n' > combining.txt
wc -c < combining.txt
wc -m < combining.txt
\end{terminal}

This has seven bytes. Under a UTF-8 locale expect six
characters, including the newline, rather than five.
On a terminal capable of displaying both forms, this file
and \texttt{accent.txt} can look the same. That doesn't
make their bytes equal. In the lab you can use the
comparison from Chapter~\ref{ch:13} to confirm that
the files differ: \cmd|cmp -s accent.txt combining.txt|
returns one. You don't need to see a difference on the
screen to use that answer. If you intend to compare the
visible text rather than compare saved bytes, first decide
which differences are meaningful to your task. Some
forms and databases require a particular text representation,
while a count for a paper label may care only about
space available for the letters. The same measurement is
unlikely to answer both questions.

While reading a real export, leave the original intact.
Save any extracts separately and name them for what
they contain. A count stored without its unit or the
command's input path is easy to misinterpret later.
When a collaborator asks how much text is present, give
the unit with the number and say whether it describes an
extract or the whole file. Neither a pager nor a counter
can infer those details for the recipient.

\section{Read two catalogs without losing the labels}
Combining two files can answer a different question from
reading each on its own. In this example the counter
receives two named operands rather than a single stream:

\begin{terminal}
wc -l -c catalog.txt accent.txt
\end{terminal}

Look for six and seventy-two on the catalog row, one
and six on the accent row, and totals of seven and
seventy-eight. Padding and the total label can differ.
Look at the column positions and name at the end of
each row. Now combine the bytes and count those instead:

\begin{terminal}
cat catalog.txt accent.txt | wc -l -c
\end{terminal}

That produces only seven and seventy-eight because the
counter gets one stream, without the two operand names.
If the first input doesn't end in a newline,
concatenating them won't insert one. Word counts can
change when a final word from one file and an initial
word from another meet without whitespace. These
fixtures deliberately end with newlines. Don't generalize
from their neat boundaries to an uninspected download.

Keep the labels when you need to diagnose a discrepancy.
One broad count can tell you how much is present but not
which part changed; two named rows identify the file that
needs inspection. In a handoff note, specify whether you
measured the files separately or the concatenated stream.
If someone repeats your command for different files,
that distinction will help them reproduce the answer.
The next chapter moves from inspecting the ends and
measuring sizes to selecting lines by their contents.

\section{Practice and cleanup}
\begin{exercise}{Find the middle without an editor}
Print just the fourth and fifth lines from the catalog.
Say which command decides where to start and which decides
where to stop. Make no changes to the source.
\end{exercise}
\begin{solution}
Use \cmd+tail -n +4 catalog.txt | head -n 2+. The first
stage starts at line four; the second limits the amount.
Expect \texttt{Field guides} and \texttt{Mystery novels}.
Neither command makes an output file here.
\end{solution}

\begin{exercise}{Compare what two counts actually measure}
Print the newline and byte counts of \texttt{accent.txt}
together. Verify whether \cmd|wc -m| provides a
third distinct number in your locale.
\end{exercise}
\begin{solution}
\cmd|wc -l -c accent.txt| shows one newline, six bytes,
and the filename. \cmd|wc -m accent.txt| shows five
characters in a UTF-8 locale. If you cannot select a UTF-8
locale, report only the verified byte and newline counts.
No additional files need removal.
\end{solution}

\begin{exercise}{Select a way to review the closing note}
If \cmd|less| is available, find the closing note using
pager navigation or search, then quit. Without a pager,
read the note using the smallest necessary tail request.
\end{exercise}
\begin{solution}
Open \cmd|less catalog.txt|, search with
\texttt{/End of list} and \EnterKey, then quit with
\texttt{q}. If there's no pager or it exits immediately,
\cmd|tail -n 1 catalog.txt| prints \texttt{End of list}.
Neither method changes the catalog or adds any files.
\end{solution}

After checking \cmd|pwd| against the recorded path, remove
only the known fixtures, with confirmation. Omit any
you didn't create. \cmd|rmdir| refuses to remove nonempty
directories; investigate any unexpected contents rather
than forcing removal. If you started a practice shell,
finish with \cmd|exit|.

\begin{terminal}
rm -i catalog.txt partial.txt accent.txt combining.txt
cd ..
rmdir "$lab"
\end{terminal}

\begin{takeaways}
\item Inspect a bounded part of a file before pouring the whole thing onto the terminal.
\item A pager is for reading, not editing or producing a filtered data set.
\item Newlines, words, bytes, characters, and display rows are not interchangeable units.
\end{takeaways}

